  // Read the 8-byte value from memory at offset localPtr + 7
  int valueFromSource = memory.getUint64(localPtr + 7);

  // Store the read value at offset 7 in the target data
  data.setUint64(7, valueFromSource);

  // Store the argument value at offset 15
  data.setUint64(15, argVal);

  // Store the localPtr value at offset 23
  data.setUint64(23, localPtr);

  // Store 0 at offset 31
  data.setUint64(31, 0);
}
```

This function assumes that `memory` is a `ByteData` instance covering the relevant address space, which is necessary because Dart doesn't allow direct memory access by address. If this is part of a larger system (e.g., native interop), you might need to adjust using `dart:ffi`, but for pure Dart, this approach abstractly captures the assembly's intent.

